{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of the H3 Mac host kit at head 2fe47de261e8e79f99de825d25cc074dc603a644, tree 1824d3e68235a09b992a46b1690bef078b35e5c6, for request cbcadd7f and review request f6989869. Verdict: APPROVED.\n\nThe head adds eight commits to main 2be6ca74 and changes 31 paths: the kit under `apps/room/celld/mac/`, the celld pin, three documents, the owner test, the implementation note and its manifest. I read the implementation note first, then the request and ruling 9fe109e9, the kit source, the documents and the native captures.\n\nThe review request and its 31 artifacts read stale. They rest on ruling 9fe109e9, and the ruling rests on question cf955132, which the builder retired at 11:50. `gs review` admits a stale basis and records it. If the merge refuses the stale chain, the planner can restate the ruling on a live base.\n\n## Against request cbcadd7f\n\n1. Units and scripts. Resolved.\n   - `install.sh`, `uninstall.sh` and `down` are thin wrappers for `kit.py` and `shutdown.py`. The three plists run as the configured user with `ExitTimeOut` 60. celld has `ThrottleInterval` 70.\n   - `service.py` applies the filer fsync rule and reads it back. It starts celld only after a store PUT succeeds, with `CELLD_TTL_MS` 60000. SeaweedFS runs with `-master.electionTimeout=1s` and an explicit `-volume.max`, and binds only to loopback.\n   - The Caddyfile keeps `grace_period 10s`, one certificate pair and `header_up CF-Connecting-IP {remote_host}`.\n   - celld's `KeepAlive` is `SuccessfulExit:false`, not plain `KeepAlive`. A preserve exit then stays stopped, and a crash still restarts. The note gives the reason, and the kill -9 witness ran under this policy.\n   - `down` disables the celld label and requests `POST /shutdown?handoff=preserve`. It waits until the original process identity closes, then stops Caddy, then the store. If closure is unproved, it keeps the store running.\n2. Certificate. Resolved. `certificate.sh` makes an EC P-256 certificate with `serverAuth`, critical `CA:FALSE`, digital-signature key usage and the given SANs. It refuses more than 825 days before it writes anything. It never overwrites an output. The repair at F removed the signing authority of the earlier `CA:TRUE` form. The owning test shows that a trusted endpoint now rejects another host's certificate signed with its key.\n3. Documents. Resolved. \"Run on a Mac\" follows the requested order: prerequisites, install, certificate and trust, first room, maintenance with `fdesetup authrestart`, then stop and remove. One paragraph each covers a user-supplied certificate and a tunnel. `docs/celld.md` links to the section. Firefox, iOS and real browser trust are marked as unverified.\n4. Witnesses on the Mac mini. Resolved.\n   - (a) `mac-clean-install-2` exits 0. `mac-first-room-run-2` creates a room through LAN HTTPS, joins a member and verifies seven journals.\n   - (b) `mac-preserve-down-3` reports `freshPrepared:true`, from a fresh `clean_reload_prepared` line in celld's own log. It also reports `nodeNotDraining:true`, `operatorStatus` 200, celld closed before the store, and `storeStopped:true`. `mac-preserve-final-recovery-verify-1` reads back all seven journals with their pre-stop prefixes. This meets ruling 9fe109e9.\n   - (c) `mac-sigkill-keepalive-1` kills celld PID 38739 after four acknowledged acts. `mac-crash-recovery-verify-1` finds all four in the recovered membership journal.\n   - (d) The policy and served-certificate captures at B, C and F accept all four SANs, refuse a wrong host and refuse 900 days.\n   - (e) `mac-https-mcp-1` reports `mcp-create-201` and `mcp-complete` through HTTPS.\n   - Every failed capture stays failed, including `mac-preserve-down-1`, `mac-preserve-down-2` and the certificate activation at F.\n5. The celld pin moves to v0.6.3 in `apps/room/celld/celld.version`, and `h1-proxy-v063-1` reruns the H1 proxy check at that pin.\n\nOn the validation daemons, `mac-clean-install-2` records `oldValidationLabelsAndPlistsAbsent:true` before it runs the installer. Its helper requires `launchctl print` to return 113 for each `dap.validate.*` label and requires each plist to be absent. The note says that it did not observe the removal commands, and no capture records them. The condition's purpose, an install from a clean state, holds. A record of who removed the daemons, and when, would complete the condition's wording. The planner can say whether the absence check is enough.\n\n## Checks at this head\n\n- The worktree is clean. `git merge-tree --write-tree` onto main 2be6ca74 gives the head tree 1824d3e6.\n- Gate checkpoint G 7a20d6ec has tree 12c71109. Only the note and the manifest change from G to the head. From F 3c9033ec to G, only the nine SDK paths of landing 2be6ca74 change. From E to F, only the certificate script, its test, `docs/self-host.md` and `docs/testing.md` change. The documents-only follow-up rule covers the tail.\n- The in-place repairs at D and E kept the installation record current. Each helper rewrote `installation.json` with the new digests and kept the prior files under `repair-history`. The E reload installed the `SuccessfulExit:false` policy before the preserve and crash sequences.\n- I reran the owner `scripts/mac-host-kit.test.mjs` once, with no whole suite. It passes: 8 Node tests and 16 Python tests.\n- The manifest has SHA-256 bed25103 and 43,102 bytes. It follows ruling 4e199bd8:\n  - all 304 listed files match by size and SHA-256;\n  - `finalization` and `operator-inputs` match their digests, and each SHA256SUMS reruns clean, with no unlisted file;\n  - the root SHA256SUMS covers all 2,167 other files, with none unlisted and none writable.\n- check-doc-links on an export of the head: 743 links, 0 broken.\n- check-notes from 2be6ca74: one warning, for the 43,102-byte manifest over the 40,960-byte soft limit. The note states the reason.\n- check-commit-messages from 2be6ca74: 8 checked, 0 flagged.\n- `git diff --check`: clean.\n\n## Points that do not block\n\n1. The maintenance steps leave the restart order to the reader. `down` disables all three labels, and the disabled state persists across a reboot. After `down` and `sudo fdesetup authrestart`, the services stay stopped until someone runs the enable and bootstrap loop. A reboot without `down` stops the services in no fixed order. Say to run `down` first, then `authrestart`, then the restart loop and `dap verify --all`.\n2. The kit has no upgrade path. The installer refuses an existing prefix. The D and E repairs used scratch helpers from the evidence to replace `shutdown.py` and the celld plist. A later slice can add an `upgrade` command that does what those helpers did.\n3. With `SuccessfulExit:false`, any exit 0 of celld leaves it stopped, and not only a preserve exit. If the pinned celld can exit 0 for another reason, the host stays down with no restart. Say so in the guide, or name the exits that are known to return 0.\n4. The loopback operator endpoint takes `POST /shutdown` with no authentication. Any local account on the Mac can stop celld. That is celld's design. The guide can say that the host should have no untrusted local accounts.\n5. Writing:\n   - The guide names Homebrew, outside the naming rule. \"Resolve symlinks in binary paths\" says the same thing.\n   - The note uses slashes in prose: \"labels/plists\", \"B/C\", \"case/module\", \"command/check\" and \"owner/control\".\n   - Several of the note's sentences are about the note itself, for example \"this note makes no LibreSSL direct-verification compatibility claim\".\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe kit is a host layer around the existing runtime. It changes no Worker, kernel, contract or wire format. `docs/architecture.md` needs no change. Each concern has one owner: `kit.py` owns rendering and installation, `service.py` owns service startup, `shutdown.py` owns ordered stop, and `deploy.py` owns the store bucket and celld deployment. The same-node preserve path follows ruling 9fe109e9. Peer handoff stays in H4. Approved.\n\n## Security\n\n- Every service runs as a configured non-root user. The installer refuses root as that user, refuses symlinks and writable parents, and never replaces a path.\n- The private key and store credentials must belong to the service user with mode 0600. Credentials reach curl through stdin, never argv. Refusal messages print no input values.\n- The store, its metrics and celld listen only on loopback. Caddy sets `CF-Connecting-IP` from the real peer, so a client cannot supply it.\n- The endpoint certificate can no longer issue certificates. The guide's keychain override changes SSL trust only.\n- Point 4 is a known local limit, not a remote one. Approved.\n\n## Simplification\n\nThe kit is 2,846 added lines, including 752 lines of tests and 228 lines of guide. The shell wrappers are four lines each. Exact checks of process identity, launchd output and log cursors carry most of the length, and the D and E failures showed each of those checks is needed. Point 2 would move the repair helpers into the kit instead of the evidence. Approved.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: approved.\n- Security: approved.\n- Simplification: approved.\n- The request's checks: parts 1 to 3 hold in the source and the documents. Witnesses (a) to (e) hold in the sealed native evidence at their own checkpoints, and the failed attempts stay failed. The evidence verifies under ruling 4e199bd8. The validation-daemon condition holds as an absence check, as stated above.\n\nThe landing check will follow the landing.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:c2db23dc135cef8afe3272fa613f03556e998312","binding":"assigned","head":"2fe47de261e8e79f99de825d25cc074dc603a644","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:cf258b4b0bd154b83a4ec39114920ae218eb5ce9\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:5e2a6e568a61cb137859a51005ea